"When Problem Solving Followed by Instruction Works: Evidence for Productive Failure - Tanmay Sinha, Manu Kapur, 2021 (https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.3102/00346543211019105)\nciteturn12view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.3102/00346543211019105\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1086\nL0: 【0†iframe†analytics.sagepub.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Skip to main content】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Open Drawer Menu Menu】\nL5: \nL6: #### Add email alerts\nL7: \nL8: You are adding the following journal to your email alerts\nL9: \nL10: [Input][Input]\nL11:  | New content\nL12: --- | ---\nL13: Review of Educational Research  | [Input]\nL14: [Button: Create email alert]\nL15: \nL16: Open access\nL17: \nL18: Research article\nL19: \nL20: First published online June 18, 2021\nL21: # When Problem Solving Followed by Instruction Works: Evidence for Productive Failure\nL22: \nL23: 【3†Tanmay Sinha】 【4†https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3069-2899†orcid.org】 【5†[email protected]】 and 【6†Manu Kapur】【7†View all authors and affiliations】\nL24: \nL25: 【8†Volume 91, Issue 5】\nL26: \nL27: 【9†https://doi.org/10.3102/00346543211019105†doi.org】\nL28: ## Abstract\nL29: When learning a new concept, should students engage in problem solving followed by instruction (PS-I) or instruction followed by problem solving (I-PS)? Noting that there is a passionate debate about the design of initial learning, we report evidence from a meta-analysis of 53 studies with 166 comparisons that compared PS-I with I-PS design. Our results showed a significant, moderate effect in favor of PS-I (Hedge’s g 0.36 [95% confidence interval 0.20; 0.51]).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1348.pdf)\nciteturn12view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"pattern\":\"Singapore\"}); Total lines: 1540\nL142@P1: students in Singapore. Our results show that StratL\nL143@P1: effectively steers the LLM to adhere to the PF tutor\u0002ing strategy and does so with no negative spillover\nL144@P1: effects on other desirable properties of the LLM.\nL145@P1: We summarize our contributions as follows:\nL146@P1: • We define the problem of Pedagogical Steer\u0002ing in the context of LLM multi-turn tutoring.\nL147@P1: We introduce the StratL algorithm to model\nL148@P1: the concept of tutoring strategy and to prompt\nL674@P8: School of Science and Technology in Singapore for\nL675@P8: supporting and hosting our field study. We thank\nL676@P8: Nico Daheim for valuable feedback and discus\u0002sions. Jakub Macina acknowledges funding from\nL677@P8: the ETH AI Center Doctoral Fellowship, Asuera\nL678@P8: Stiftung, and the ETH Zurich Foundation.\nL679@P8: References\nL680@P8: Mona Alzahrani, Manal Alharbi, and Amani Alod\u0002wani. 2019. The effect of social-emotional compe\u0002tence on children academic achievement and behav\u0002ioral development. International Education Studies,\nL681@P8: 12(12):141–149.\nL711@P9: Manu Kapur. 2016. Examining productive failure, pro\u0002ductive success, unproductive failure, and unproduc\u0002tive success in learning. Educational Psychologist,\nL712@P9: 51(2):289–299.\nL713@P9: Manu Kapur. 2024. Productive Failure: Unlocking\nL714@P9: Deeper Learning Through the Science of Failing. Wi\u0002ley.\nL715@P9: Manu Kapur and Katerine Bielaczyc. 2012. Design\u0002ing for productive failure. Journal of the Learning\nL716@P9: Sciences, 21(1):45–83.\nL717@P9: Manu Kapur and Nikol Rummel. 2012. Productive\nL718@P9: failure in learning from generation and invention ac\u0002tivities. Instructional Science, 40(4):645–650.\nL719@P9: Manu Kapur and Leslie Toh. 2015. Learning from Pro\u0002ductive Failure, pages 213–227. Springer Singapore,\nL720@P9: Singapore.\nL721@P9: Enkelejda Kasneci, Kathrin Sessler, Stefan Küche\u0002mann, Maria Banne