of the Learning\nL775@P10: Sciences.\nL776@P10: Douglas C. Merrill, Brian J. Reiser, Michael Ranney,\nL777@P10: and J. Gregory Trafton. 1992. Effective tutoring tech\u0002niques: A comparison of human tutors and intelligent\nL778@P10: tutoring systems. The Journal of the Learning Sci\u0002ences, 2(3):277–305.\nL779@P10: Grégoire Mialon, Roberto Dessi, Maria Lomeli, Christo\u0002foros Nalmpantis, Ramakanth Pasunuru, Roberta\nL780@P10: Raileanu, Baptiste Roziere, Timo Schick, Jane\nL781@P10: Dwivedi-Yu, Asli Celikyilmaz, Edouard Grave, Yann\nL782@P10: LeCun, and Thomas Scialom. 2023. Augmented lan\u0002guage models: a survey. Transactions on Machine\nL783@P10: Learning Research. Survey Certification.\nL784@P10: Phillip M. Newman and Marci S. DeCaro. 2019. Learn\u0002ing by exploring: How much guidance is optimal?\nL785@P10: Learning and Instruction, 62:49–63.\nL786@P10: OpenAi. 2024. Prompt engineering.\nL787@P10: https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/prompt\u0002engineering/. Accessed: 2024-03.\nL788@P10: Long Ouyang, Jeffrey Wu, Xu Jiang, Diogo Almeida,\nL829@P10: solving followed by instruction works: evidence for\nL830@P10: productive failure. Review of Educational Research,\nL831@P10: 91(5):761–798.\nL832@P10: Shashank Sonkar, Naiming Liu, Debshila Mallick, and\nL833@P10: Richard Baraniuk. 2023. CLASS: A design frame\u0002work for building intelligent tutoring systems based\nL834@P10: on learning science principles. In Findings of the\nL835@P10: Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP\nL836@P10: 2023, pages 1941–1961, Singapore. Association for\nL837@P10: Computational Linguistics.\nL838@P10: Anaïs Tack and Chris Piech. 2022. The ai teacher test:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1348.pdf)\nciteturn12view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"pattern\":\"complete learning\"}); Total lines: 1540\nNo matching text found for \"complete learning\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1348.pdf)\nciteturn12view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"pattern\":\"proxy\"}); Total lines: 1540\nL416@P5: (“Ask for Next Step” in Table 5 of Appendix B.1)\nL417@P5: that only asks the student to proceed with the exer\u0002cise. We use this baseline to assess the importance\nL418@P5: of the Intent Selection procedure. V4: LLM In\u0002tent use an LLM to perform the Intent Selection\nL419@P5: (with the prompt given in Appendix B.2.3) Wang\nL420@P5: et al. (2024b), and we use it to answer DQ1.\nL421@P5: 4.4 Evaluation Metrics\nL422@P5: To quantitatively assess the PF fidelity of the dif\u0002ferent versions (their success in following PF), we\nL423@P5: have to rely on proxy variables. We propose as\nL424@P5: a main proxy the number of student-generated\nL425@P5: Representation and Solution Methods (RSMs),\nL426@P5: which can be seen as the different ways students\nL427@P5: represent the problem and try to solve it. As ex\u0002plained in Section 2.3, the diversity of student\u0002generated RSMs is the first predictor of learning\nL428@P5: gains with PF (Kapur and Bielaczyc, 2012; Kapur\nL429@P5: and Rummel, 2012). We thus use this number to\nL430@P5: measure the PF fidelity of the tutor.\nL431@P5: However, not every problem easily admits mul\u0002tiple RSMs. In our case, only Consistency does.\nL432@P5: We thus define another proxy, the PF Score. One\nL433@P5: of the core ideas of PF is to encourage the student\nL434@P5: to construct their own understanding of the prob\u0002lem by exploring it by themselves, even if they do\nL435@P5: not succeed in solving it. Building on this idea, we\nL436@P5: define for each problem a grading rubric used to\nL437@P5: compute the tutor’s PF Score: For each problem,\nL438@P5: we identify a list of critical reasoning steps that\nL439@P5: represent different conceptual understandings of\nL440@P5: the problem. To assess a tutoring conversation, we\nL441@P5: give a point to the tutor for every crucial reason\u0002ing step that the student found on their own. We\nL442@P5: do not award the point if the tutor reveals it or di\u0002rectly hints to it. This metric can be thought of as\nL443@P5: an adjusted sub-step success rate. An efficient PF\nL444@P5: tutor would help the student explore the problem\nL445@P5: and thus achieve these conceptual understandings,\nL446@P5: without giving them any direct hint or revealing\nL447@P5: them, resulting in a high PF Score. We display\nL448@P5: the precise sub-steps for both testing problems in\nL449@P5: Appendix A.3.\nL450@P5: 4.5 Preliminary Study with Simulated\nL451@P5: Students\nL452@P5: Due to ethical concerns (Kasneci et al., 2023), we\nL453@P5: first tested our prototype with GPT-4–simulated\nL454@P5: students before involving humans. This method\nL455@P5: of using simulated students, known as interactive\nL456@P5: tutoring, is used in the literature (Macina et al.,\nL457@P5: 2023a; Sonkar et al., 2023). Importantly, we do\nL458@P5: not assume that the LLM can accurately model stu\u0002dents’ learning. However, this assumption is not\nL459@P5: necessary for our preliminary study as we are in\u0002terested in the ability of our algorithm to follow a\nL460@P5-6: 26296PF tutoring strategy, rather than the learning gains\nL461@P6: it produces. We simulated three conversations for\nL462@P6: each of two problems across four tutor versions\nL463@P6: (V1–4) and used a scoring rubric to annotate con\u0002versations, computing the PF Score and RSM count\nL503@P6: RQ1. The strategy followed by an LLM con\u0002trolled by StratL is determined by the Intent Selec\u0002tion procedure. Overall, V3 achieves a rather high\nL504@P6: PF Score on the two problems, since it never pro\u0002vides any hint to students. However, this result is\nL505@P6: more a consequence of the way we defined the PF\nL506@P6: score rather than a sign of PF fidelity, as V3 does\nL507@P6: not perform as well on our second proxy: It does\nL508@P6: not manage to elicit as many RSMs from students\nL509@P6: as V1, with an average of only 1.34. This shows\nL510@P6: that the strategy followed by an LLM controlled by\nL511@P6: StratL is determined by the Intent Selection proce\u0002dure as it is the only part that differs (V1 vs. V3).\nL512@P6: Based on our preliminary answers to DQ1 and\nL513@P6: RQ1, we decided not to include V3 and V4 in\nL514@P6: our field test with real students to focus on the\nL515@P6: best-performing version (V1) while keeping the\nL516@P6: non-StratL baseline (V2) for comparison.\nL517@P6: Accuracy of Student State Tracing The func\u0002tioning of StratL as a whole is contingent upon the\nL518@P6: functioning of the Student State Tracing. To test its\nL519@P6: reliability, we generate ten tutoring conversations\nL520@P6: for both problems and the first author manually an\u0002notates them with ground truth state features st\nL521@P6: . We\nL522@P6: compare the outputs of the Student State Tracing\nL523@P6: with the annotated data. As a multi-label classifi\u0002cation task, we compute for each state feature the\nL524@P6: precision, recall, and F1 score. We compare three\nL525@P6: different implementations of the State Tracing step\nL526@P6: and provide more details about our methodology\nL527@P6: and results in Table 4 of Appendix A.4. Our imple\u0002mentation achieves a satisfactory micro F1 score\nL528@P6: of 0.77. The Student State Tracing is a task of its\nL529@P6: own, and we encourage researchers to try different\nL530@P6: classification models to improve its reliability.\nL531@P6: 5.2 Field Test with Students: Measuring\nL532@P6: end-to-end Effectiveness\nL533@P6: RQ1. Tutoring Strategy Fidelity StratL is suc\u0002cessful in steering the LLM to follow the PF tutor\u0002ing strategy. We report in Figure 3 the results of\nL534@P6: the protocol defined in Section 4.4. We note the PF\nL535@P6: score (out of 4) is used as a proxy for the extent\nL536@P6: to which the tutor followed the PF tutoring strat\u0002egy. We also report the average number of student\u0002generated RSMs (partial solutions) on the problem\nL537@P6: Consistency. This metric is best proxy for PF fi\u0002delity, as one of the goals of the PF tutoring strategy\nL538@P6: is to maximize this number, as explained in Section\nL539@P6: 2.3. Out of the two test problems, the number of\nL540@P6-7: 26297Figure 3: Field test results and their confidence intervals.\nL541@P7: ‘PF.S’ refers to the ‘PF Score’ as defined in Section 4.4\nL542@P7: and is out of 4. ‘RSMs’ denotes the number of student\u0002generated RSMs. StratL (V1) succeeds in steering the\nL543@P7: LLM to follow PF as compared to V2.\nL544@P7: N Coherence Empathy Helpful.\nL545@P7: Problem: Consistency\nL546@P7: V1. 5 1.80 1.60 1.20\nL547@P7: V2 4 2.25 1.75 2.00\nL548@P7: Problem: Country\nL549@P7: V1 3 3.00 2.67 2.67\nL550@P7: V2. 5 3.00 2.20 2.80\nL551@P7: Table 2: Feedback questionnaire results (out of 3). ‘N’\nL552@P7: refers to the number of answers to the questionnaire.\nL553@P7: We do not detect any harmful effect of StratL (V1) on\nL554@P7: the LLM tutor’s coherence, empathy, or perceived help\u0002fulness.\nL555@P7: RSMs can only be measured on Consistency as it\nL556@P7: is the only invention problem.\nL557@P7: On both test problems, the StratL-controlled\nL558@P7: LLM (V1) achieved a higher (statistically signif\u0002icant for problem Consistency, p = .046 with a\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.1348.pdf)\nciteturn12view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view0\",\"pattern\":\"consolidation\"}); Total lines: 1540\nNo matching text found for \"consolidation\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Teach Programming in the AI Era? Using LLMs as a Teachable Agent for Debugging (https://arxiv.org/html/2310.05292v3)\nciteturn12view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view2\",\"pattern\":\"30-minute\"}); Total lines: 251\nNo matching text found for \"30-minute\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Teach Programming in the AI Era? Using LLMs as a Teachable Agent for Debugging (https://arxiv.org/html/2310.05292v3)\nciteturn12view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view2\",\"pattern\":\"counterbalanced\"}); Total lines: 251\nL175: Our final tests are based on two programming exercises with comparable difficulties. We counterbalanced pre-post tests’ problems to control for problem sequence influence. Each test consists of seven questions, with three assessing 【57†LO1】 and four for 【58†LO2】. 【71†Figure 6】 provides a sample for each. For instance, Question 3.1 asks students to identify the more suitable test case to add to an existing test suite, evaluating their ability to construct comprehensive hypotheses (【57†LO1】).\nL176: We measure students’ performance using their test scores based on a standard rubric. We also log the pre-post tests’ completion time as a proxy for proficiency.\nL177: #### Method: Study Procedure and Participants.\nL178: Our hour-long user study constituted a pre-survey, pre-test, interaction with HypoCompass, post-test, and a post-survey. Participants began with a pre-survey, which asked demographic information and 7-level Likert Scale questions on their debugging experiences. Then, participants had up to 20 minutes for the pre-test. The system interaction consisted of two problems, where participants needed to write a test suite and explain bugs in three different buggy programs for each problem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning-by-teaching with ChatGPT: The effect of teachable ChatGPT agent on programming education (https://arxiv.org/html/2412.15226v1)\nciteturn12view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view3\",\"pattern\":\"random\"}); Total lines: 338\nNo matching text found for \"random\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning-by-teaching with ChatGPT: The effect of teachable ChatGPT agent on programming education (https://arxiv.org/html/2412.15226v1)\nciteturn12view8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view3\",\"pattern\":\"Table 2\"}); Total lines: 338\nL162: The ANCOVA results showed that the three groups were significantly different in knowledge test scores (F = 35.54, p < 0.05, $\\eta$2 = 0.74) (as shown in Table 【107†2】) that EG had a higher adjusted average score than CG (11.86 > 10.53). In other words, learners who learning AI knowledge with teaching the ChatGPT teachable agent have better knowledge gains than those who learning algorithm knowledge with online videos.\nL163: Table 2: ANCOVA results of knowledge test scores\nL164: Group  | N  | Mean  | SD  | Adjusted Mean  | Adjusted SE  | F  | $\\eta$^{2}\nL165: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL166: EG  | 20  | 11.90  | 2.09  | 11.86  | 0.32  | 35.54***  | 0.74\nL167: CG  | 21  | 10.76  | 1.02  | 10.53  | 0.24\nL168: ### 4.2 Programming test scores\nL169: Before examining the impact of the ChatGPT teachable agent approach on participants’ programming skills, Levene’s test for equality of error variances showed comparable variance of the two groups (F = 2.93, p > 0.05), confirming that the variances were homogeneous. Additionally, the homogeneity of regression coefficients within the group was confirmed by the intra-group regression coefficient homogeneity test (F = 0.13, p > 0.05), allowing for the use of ANCOVA.\nL170: The ANCOVA results (See Table 【108†3】) revealed that participants in the experimental group, who learned programming with the teachable agent, scored significantly higher in code clearness and readability compared to the control group, which learned through online videos (F = 7.39, p < 0.01, $\\eta^{2}$ = 0.37). However, there was no significant difference in code correctness between the two groups (F = 2.98, p > 0.05, $\\eta^{2}$ = 0.19).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLearning-by-teaching with ChatGPT: The effect of teachable ChatGPT agent on programming education (https://arxiv.org/html/2412.15226v1)\nciteturn12view9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn7view3\",\"pattern\":\"correctness\"}); Total lines: 338\nL146: The pseudocodes were evaluated by two experienced scorers, focusing on three key aspects: clearness, correctness, and readability [【103†67】] (See detailed standard in Table 【104†1】). To ensure consistency in scoring, Spearman’s correlation analysis was performed, revealing high-rate agreement across all three dimensions, with correlation coefficients of 0.833, 0.935, and 0.911, all statistically significant (p < 0.001).\nL147: Following the scoring process, the scorers engaged in cross-checking and discussions to resolve any discrepancies, ultimately agreeing on the final evaluation scores.\nL148: Table 1: Pseudocode test scoring standard\nL149: Dimension  | Description  | Score\nL150: --- | --- | ---\nL151: Clearness  | Clearness involves the logical flow and structure of the pseudocode, ensuring that the steps of the algorithm are straightforward and unambiguous.  | 5\nL152: Correctness  | Correctness is determined by the ability of the pseudocode to solve the “eight queens\" puzzle as intended, ensuring that all conditions and rules of the puzzle are met without errors.  | 5\nL153: Readability  | Refers to how easily the pseudocode can be read and interpreted, particularly in terms of formatting, consistency, and use of natural language-like constructs. Readability involves using clear variable names, consistent indentation, and comments or explanations that enhance understanding.  | 5\nL154: #### 3.4.3 SRL ability test\nL155: The SRL ability was measured using an adapted version of the SRL questionnaire, which comprised 20 items rated on a five-point Likert scale. This questionnaire is derived from the Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire (MSLQ) [【105†68】], a widely recognized SRL measurement grounded in social cognitive theory [【106†69】]. The MSLQ includes two dimensions, namely motivation and strategy.\nL170: The ANCOVA results (See Table 【108†3】) revealed that participants in the experimental group, who learned programming with the teachable agent, scored significantly higher in code clearness and readability compared to the control group, which learned through online videos (F = 7.39, p < 0.01, $\\eta^{2}$ = 0.37). However, there was no significant difference in code correctness between the two groups (F = 2.98, p > 0.05, $\\eta^{2}$ = 0.19).\nL172: Dimension  | Group  | N  | Mean  | SD  | Adjusted Mean  | Adjusted SE  | F  | $\\eta$^{2}\nL173: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL174: Clearness  | EG  | 20  | 4.20  | 0.61  | 4.13  | 0.05  | 7.39***  | 0.37\nL175: CG  | 21  | 3.29  | 0.90  | 3.17  | 0.01\nL176: Correctness  | EG  | 20  | 3.81  | 0.96  | 3.79  | 0.10  | 2.98  | 0.19\nL177: CG  | 21  | 3.90  | 1.47  | 3.82  | 0.03\nL178: Clearness  | EG  | 20  | 2.91  | 1.23  | 2.95  | 0.03  | 4.32**  | 0.26\nL179: CG  | 21  | 2.81  | 0.58  | 2.83  | 0.02\nL180: \nL181:   * •\nL209: Our results revealed that learners in the experimental group (EG) achieved higher scores in programming tests related to code clearness and readability, while there were no significant differences in code correctness compared to the control group (CG). In our learning task, code clearness refers to whether learners provided a logical flow in their coding, and readability pertains to the ease with which the code could be understood, including the use of natural language comments.\nL210: These qualities are recognized as markers of high-quality code [【114†74】]. Our findings suggest that by guiding a ChatGPT teachable agent in code generation, learners were able to refine their coding logic and develop a deeper understanding of what constitutes easily readable code from the perspective of the reader, rather than just the programmer.\nL211: This indicates that the process of instructing the ChatGPT agent helped learners to clarify their thought processes in coding, ultimately contributing to their development of more structured and comprehensible code. Moreover, this learning by teaching approach likely encouraged learners to prioritize the readability and clearness of their code, emphasizing the importance of writing code that is understandable to others [【115†75】].\nL212: However, our findings indicate that students using ChatGPT agents for teaching did not show significant differences in code correctness compared to the control group (CG), with the CG actually achieving slightly higher scores in code correctness. In other words, learners who interacted with ChatGPT agents were likely to produce code with a variety of bugs in the testing phase.\nL213: One possible explanation is that ChatGPT agents can be ruled towards generating fully correct code (part of the ChatGPT pre-training that forced to generate correct codes) when responding to learners’ guidance [【116†76】], which deprives learners of the opportunity to practice determining code correctness themselves, such as finding bugs in the code. In contrast, the CG, which required students to write their own code, provided more opportunities to practice identifying bugs in their code.\nL214: Data from the backend auto-judging platform also revealed that the experimental group (EG) had an average of 1.95 code submission attempts, whereas the CG had an average of 2.90 attempts, indicating that the experimental group passed the auto-judgement with fewer attempts.\nL215: This finding was echoed previous studies that although the latest ChatGPT models have strong code generation accuracy [【48†18】], there is a need for precise control of the teachable agents’ cognitive behaviors (e.g., knowledge levels and question-asking) to facilitate the intended learning experience [【117†77】, 【118†78】] . Our results indicate that for ChatGPT-based teachable agents, those that occasionally generate incorrect responses may better promote the practice of error correction among learners.\nL216: In other words, more advanced language models like GPT-4, with their extensive knowledge, may be less effective in developing learners’ practical skills in error correction compared to more modest models like GPT-3.5, which can occasionally generate errors for learners to correct.\n"
